window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"][6]={"university_biology_en:M6-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M6-L1 – DNA and chromatin<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Molecular Genetics and Gene Regulation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L3<\/a>: functional groups; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1<\/a>: cell types; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L1<\/a>: protein structure.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Orient DNA strands, determine\ncomplementary sequences and explain eukaryotic genome packaging as a\ndynamic organisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A chromosome must protect hereditary information while keeping it\navailable for copying and selective access. DNA chemistry and its\nspatial organisation address different parts of this challenge.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"molecular-architecture\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Molecular architecture<\/h3>\n<p>A deoxyribonucleotide contains deoxyribose, phosphate and a base\n(adenine, thymine, guanine or cytosine). Phosphodiester bonds connect\nthe 3′ oxygen of one sugar through phosphate to the next sugar’s 5′ end.\nBackbone polarity defines a reading direction. The two strands are\nantiparallel. A pairs with T and G with C; base pairing and stacking\ninteractions contribute to double-helix stability. Major and minor\ngrooves expose chemical features of bases to proteins. Negatively\ncharged phosphates interact with ions and basic proteins.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"chromatin-and-accessibility\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Chromatin and accessibility<\/h3>\n<p>In eukaryotic nuclei, approximately 147 base pairs wrap around a\nhistone octamer containing two copies each of H2A, H2B, H3 and H4.\nLinker DNA between nucleosomes varies in length. Histone H1 can assist\nlinker organisation but is not part of the core octamer. Chromatin forms\nvariable contacts, loops and domains rather than a rigid universal\npackaging hierarchy. ATP-dependent remodelers can reposition\nnucleosomes. Accessible regions are frequently associated with gene\nactivity, but accessibility alone does not ensure transcription.<\/p>\n<p>Bacterial chromosomes are organised through supercoiling and\nDNA-binding proteins. Archaea and organelles exhibit further forms of\norganisation. Not all DNA is contained in linear, histone-packaged\nnuclear chromosomes.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_028_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Base pairing with opposite strand polarity and schematic DNA packaging around histone octamers; not to scale.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Base pairing with opposite strand\npolarity and schematic DNA packaging around histone octamers; not to\nscale.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Base pairing with opposite strand polarity and schematic\nDNA packaging around histone octamers; not to scale.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A model sequence is 5′-AGTCGA-3′. Its opposite strand is\n3′-TCAGCT-5′; in its own 5′→3′ direction it reads 5′-TCGACT-3′. Three of\nsix base pairs are G/C pairs, giving 50%. For 1,000 bp of extended\nB-DNA, an approximate rise of 0.34 nm per base pair gives a contour\nlength of 340 nm. This is not the diameter of the corresponding\nchromatin region.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Determine the reverse complement of 5′-ATGCC-3′.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Estimate the contour length of 3,000 bp of B-DNA.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the presence of nucleosomes alone not determine gene\nactivity?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">5′-GGCAT-3′.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3000<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.34<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1020<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3000\\cdot0.34=1020<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnm, or 1.02 µm.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Positioning, modifications, regulatory proteins and cellular context\ninfluence accessibility and transcription.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The figure is schematic and not to scale. Other DNA conformations and\nlocal distortions differ from idealised B-DNA. Sequence composition\nalone determines neither melting temperature nor chromatin state\ncompletely.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Complementarity enables DNA copying; spatial organisation influences\nits use. M6-L2 covers copying and maintenance of the genome.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/14-2-dna-structure-and-sequencing\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 14.2 DNA Structure and Sequencing<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · DNA structure and\npackaging · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/16-3-eukaryotic-epigenetic-gene-regulation\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 16.3 Eukaryotic Epigenetic Gene Regulation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chromatin\naccessibility · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M6-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M6-L2 – Replication, repair and recombination<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Molecular Genetics and Gene Regulation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L1<\/a>: DNA polarity; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L5<\/a>: cell cycle.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive replication directions\nand distinguish proofreading, repair and homologous recombination.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A cell must duplicate its DNA before division. Copying accuracy and\nrestoration of damaged molecules are separate but coupled tasks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"replication\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Replication<\/h3>\n<p>Semiconservative replication produces duplexes containing one old and\none newly formed strand each. At origins, helicases open the duplex,\ntopoisomerases relieve torsional stress and single-strand-binding\nproteins stabilise exposed DNA. DNA polymerases extend an existing 3′-OH\nend and thus require a primer. Synthesis always proceeds 5′→3′. At a\nmoving fork, the leading strand grows towards the fork, while the\nlagging strand grows in separate Okazaki fragments away from it. Primers\nare removed and replaced; ligase seals remaining backbone nicks.<\/p>\n<p>Eukaryotic chromosomes use many regulated origins. Linear chromosome\nends require specialised maintenance mechanisms; telomerase can extend\ntelomeric DNA in appropriate cells. Its activity depends on cell\ntype.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"genome-maintenance\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Genome maintenance<\/h3>\n<p>Proofreading removes some incorrect nucleotides during synthesis.\nMismatch repair corrects escaped mismatches. Base excision repair\nreplaces certain chemically altered bases; nucleotide excision repair\nremoves a short segment containing particular helix-distorting lesions.\nDouble-strand breaks may be repaired by homologous recombination using a\nhomologous template or by end joining. Recombination also allows\nexchange of homologous chromosome segments in meiosis. These pathways\ndiffer in template requirements and potential errors; none is\nuniversally error-free.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_029_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"At a rightward-moving fork, the leading strand is synthesised rightwards and lagging-strand fragments leftwards.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">At a rightward-moving fork, the leading\nstrand is synthesised rightwards and lagging-strand fragments\nleftwards.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: At a rightward-moving fork, the leading strand is\nsynthesised rightwards and lagging-strand fragments leftwards.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A simplified labelling experiment begins with fully heavy-labelled\nDNA and changes to a medium containing only light precursor. After one\ncomplete replication cycle all duplexes are hybrids. After two cycles,\nhalf are hybrids and half fully light. After three cycles, the hybrid\nfraction is 1/4. The two originally heavy single strands persist while\ntotal duplex number doubles each cycle.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In which direction does DNA polymerase extend a strand?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What hybrid fraction follows after four cycles in the model?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a homologous template useful for double-strand break\nrepair?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">5′→3′ by extending the 3′ end.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>16<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12.5<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2/16=1/8=12.5\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Its matching sequence can supply information to restore the damaged\nsegment.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The labelling model assumes synchronous complete replication without\nexchange of labelled bases. The diagram shows a single fork; the actual\ncomposition of the replication complex varies among organisms. Repair\npathways are covered mechanistically, not as laboratory protocols.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Replication duplicates DNA; transcription uses selected regions as\ntemplates for RNA.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/14-4-dna-replication-in-prokaryotes\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 14.4 DNA Replication in Prokaryotes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Replication\nfork, primers and strand synthesis · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/14-5-dna-replication-in-eukaryotes\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 14.5 DNA Replication in Eukaryotes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Origins and\ntelomeres · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/14-6-dna-repair\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 14.6 DNA Repair<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Proofreading and repair pathways ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary\">NHGRI – Talking\nGlossary of Genetic Terms<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Homologous recombination and genetic\nvariation · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M6-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M6-L3 – Transcription and RNA processing<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Molecular Genetics and Gene Regulation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L1<\/a>: sequence and\nchromatin; <a href=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L2<\/a>: template\nprinciple.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish template and coding\nstrands and explain eukaryotic RNA processing and alternative splice\nforms.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Gene expression often starts with production of an RNA molecule. The\nRNA can perform a function itself or serve as an intermediate in protein\nsynthesis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"transcription\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transcription<\/h3>\n<p>RNA polymerases read the DNA template strand 3′→5′ and synthesise RNA\n5′→3′ without a DNA primer. RNA uses uracil instead of thymine. For the\nsame region, the RNA sequence matches the coding DNA strand with U\nreplacing T. Which DNA strand is used as the template depends on the\ngene. Promoters and regulatory factors determine initiation. Elongation\nextends the transcript; termination ends the process. In eukaryotes RNA\npolymerase II mainly produces pre-mRNA, while polymerases I and III\nproduce various ribosomal and transfer RNAs, among other products.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"maturation-and-isoforms\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Maturation and isoforms<\/h3>\n<p>Many eukaryotic pre-mRNAs acquire a modified 5′ cap supporting\nstability, export and translation. The spliceosome removes introns and\njoins exons. At the 3′ end the transcript is frequently cleaved and\ngiven a poly(A) tail, which is not simply a copy of a long DNA A\nsequence. Alternative splice-site selection can produce different mature\ntranscripts from one gene. Not every exon encodes protein: untranslated\nregions can also be exonic. Control mechanisms influence RNA export and\ndegradation. Bacteria lack many typical eukaryotic pre-mRNA maturation\nsteps; transcription and translation can be spatially coupled.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_030_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three exons in pre-mRNA; isoform A retains all three, isoform B skips E2. Both mature transcripts are shown schematically with cap and poly(A).\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three exons in pre-mRNA; isoform A\nretains all three, isoform B skips E2. Both mature transcripts are shown\nschematically with cap and poly(A).<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Three exons in pre-mRNA; isoform A retains all three,\nisoform B skips E2. Both mature transcripts are shown schematically with\ncap and poly(A).<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A fictional transcript contains exons E1 = 120 nt, E2 = 90 nt, E3 =\n150 nt and two introns totalling 700 nt. Excluding cap and poly(A), the\npre-mRNA is 1,060 nt. Isoform A contains E1–E2–E3 and measures 360 nt;\nisoform B skips E2 and measures 270 nt. Protein length cannot be\ninferred from this alone because exons can include untranslated\nsequences.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Write the RNA corresponding to template 3′-TACGGT-5′.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How long would the mature RNA be if only E1 and E2 remained?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why do distinct RNA isoforms not prove that distinct stable proteins\nexist?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">5′-AUGCCA-3′.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>120<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>90<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>210<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">120+90=210<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnt, excluding cap and poly(A).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Reading frame, translation, RNA stability and protein degradation\ncan differ; some isoforms are noncoding.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The numbers are invented and the diagram is simplified. Not all RNA\ntypes are processed identically; not every eukaryotic gene contains\nintrons. Transcript quantities alone do not directly establish protein\nactivity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Mature RNA can serve as a template for the ribosome. M6-L4 explains\npolypeptide production.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-3-eukaryotic-transcription\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 15.3 Eukaryotic Transcription<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · RNA polymerases and\ntranscription · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-4-rna-processing-in-eukaryotes\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 15.4 RNA Processing in Eukaryotes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Capping, splicing\nand polyadenylation · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M6-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M6-L4 – Translation and protein regulation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Molecular Genetics and Gene Regulation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L3<\/a>: RNA; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L1<\/a>: proteins; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L2<\/a>: enzymes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Translate a short mRNA using the\nstandard code and explain the steps from ribosome to regulated\nprotein.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>An mRNA sequence does not supply a ready-made protein structure. The\ncell must convert a nucleotide language into an amino acid sequence,\nthen fold, localise and sometimes modify the product.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"the-genetic-code\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The genetic code<\/h3>\n<p>Every three nucleotides form a codon in the defined reading frame.\nThe standard code has 64 codons: 61 specify amino acids and three signal\ntermination (UAA, UAG, UGA). Several codons can specify the same amino\nacid, but in the usual context a codon does not arbitrarily specify\nmultiple amino acids. AUG specifies methionine and frequently serves as\na start codon. The initiation site establishes the reading frame.\nOrganelles and some organisms have variant codes; special signals can\nchange the meaning of individual stop codons, among other effects.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"translation-and-maturation\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Translation and maturation<\/h3>\n<p>Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases attach appropriate amino acids to tRNAs\nusing ATP. In the ribosome, codon and anticodon are checked in\nantiparallel orientation. The A site usually accepts a new\naminoacyl-tRNA, the P site holds the growing chain and the E site allows\nuncharged tRNA to exit. Ribosomal RNA catalyses peptide-bond formation;\nthe chain grows from its N to C terminus. Elongation factors and GTP\nsupport selection and translocation. At a stop codon, release factors\nmediate polypeptide release. The small and large subunits can be\nreused.<\/p>\n<p>Chaperones, targeting signals and post-translational modifications\ncontrol folding, transport and activity. Phosphorylation can activate or\ninhibit a protein; ubiquitin tags may direct proteasomal degradation,\namong other functions. Protein concentration and functional activity are\nnot equivalent.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_031_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic state before peptide-bond formation, with uncharged tRNA at E, peptidyl-tRNA at P and aminoacyl-tRNA at A.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic state before peptide-bond\nformation, with uncharged tRNA at E, peptidyl-tRNA at P and\naminoacyl-tRNA at A.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Schematic state before peptide-bond formation, with\nuncharged tRNA at E, peptidyl-tRNA at P and aminoacyl-tRNA at\nA.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>For artificial mRNA 5′-AUG GCU UUU GGA UAA-3′, the standard code\nyields Met–Ala–Phe–Gly, then stop. Four amino acids are connected by\nthree peptide bonds. A highly simplified energy budget per additional\namino acid comprises two ATP equivalents for tRNA charging (ATP to AMP)\nand about two GTP for elongation steps. Initiation, termination and\nquality control require additional energy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many amino acids does AUG GCU UAA yield in the standard code\nwith initiation at AUG?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many peptide bonds occur in an unbranched 100-amino-acid\npolypeptide?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why might protein activity decline even if its concentration is\nconstant?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two: methionine and alanine.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">99.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example, an inhibitory modification, conformational change or\nloss of a required cofactor.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The diagram depicts an idealised elongation state, not atomic\nstructure. The short model sequence is invented. The calculation\nexcludes removal of initiating methionine and subsequent processing;\nenergy expenditure depends on the system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The effects of DNA changes depend on the reading frame, RNA and\nprotein function. M6-L5 addresses these variants.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 15.1 The Genetic Code<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Codons and reading frames ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-5-ribosomes-and-protein-synthesis\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 15.5 Ribosomes and Protein Synthesis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Translation and\nribosomes · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/16-6-eukaryotic-translational-and-post-translational-gene-regulation\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 16.6 Eukaryotic Translational and Post-translational Gene\nRegulation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Protein regulation · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M6-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M6-L5 – Mutations and genetic variation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Molecular Genetics and Gene Regulation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L2<\/a>: repair; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L4<\/a>: genetic code; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a>: proportions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Classify genetic variants by\nsequence change and potential functional effect, and distinguish allele\nfrequencies from disease risks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Variation underlies biological diversity. A change relative to a\nreference sequence is not automatically harmful; its effect depends on\nlocation, biological context and environment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"types-and-origins\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Types and origins<\/h3>\n<p>A substitution replaces a base. Transitions exchange purine for\npurine (A/G) or pyrimidine for pyrimidine (C/T); transversions exchange\nbases between the classes. Insertions and deletions change sequence\nlength. Other variants affect larger segments, including duplications,\ninversions, translocations or copy-number changes. DNA damage initially\ncomprises chemical or structural alterations; permanently fixed sequence\nchanges constitute mutations. Mutations can arise from replication\nerrors, spontaneous chemical processes or damaging influences. They do\nnot occur specifically because an organism needs an adaptation.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"function-and-inheritance\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Function and inheritance<\/h3>\n<p>In protein-coding sequence a substitution can be synonymous, specify\nanother amino acid (missense) or produce a premature stop codon\n(nonsense). Indels with lengths not divisible by three shift the reading\nframe in coding regions. Even synonymous variants can influence RNA\nprocessing or translation. Noncoding variants may alter promoters,\nsplice sites or regulatory RNAs. A germline change can be transmitted to\noffspring; somatic variants mainly pass through the affected cell\nlineage. In plants and other organisms the germline–soma distinction\ndoes not always follow the animal pattern.<\/p>\n<p>Functional classification requires consideration of segregation,\npopulation frequency, molecular data and experimental evidence together.\nA reference allele is not necessarily the most common or functionally\noptimal allele.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_032_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"GAA specifies glutamate; GAG is synonymous, GUA specifies valine, and UAA terminates translation in the standard code.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">GAA specifies glutamate; GAG is\nsynonymous, GUA specifies valine, and UAA terminates translation in the\nstandard code.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: GAA specifies glutamate; GAG is synonymous, GUA specifies\nvaline, and UAA terminates translation in the standard code.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>In model mRNA codon 5′-GAA-3′, the standard code specifies glutamate.\nGAG is synonymous, GUA gives valine and UAA is a stop signal. A second\nfictional example considers 50 diploid individuals with 30 AA, 15 Aa and\n5 aa. The frequency of a is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(15+2\\cdot5)/(2\\cdot50)=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe aa genotype frequency, however, is 5/50 = 10%. Neither quantity\ngives disease probability without phenotype information.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does a three-nucleotide deletion in a coding region necessarily\nshift the reading frame?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the frequency of A in the model.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why might a noncoding variant have a functional effect?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; it changes sequence but does not permanently shift the\ndownstream reading frame.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2\\cdot30+15)/100=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It may change transcription rate, RNA stability or splice sites, for\nexample.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The examples are artificial and cannot be interpreted clinically.\nEffects may depend on cell type, gene interactions and environment.\nSmall samples and sequence comparisons alone cannot establish a reliable\nfunctional or risk classification.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>M6-L6 examines differences in gene activity that do not necessarily\nrequire a DNA sequence change.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L6<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Mutation\">NHGRI –\nMutation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sequence changes, somatic and germline variation · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 15.1 The Genetic Code<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Codons and reading frames ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/14-6-dna-repair\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 14.6 DNA Repair<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Proofreading and repair pathways ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M6-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M6-L6 – Gene regulation and epigenetics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Molecular Genetics and Gene Regulation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L1<\/a>: chromatin; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L3<\/a>: RNA; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L4<\/a>: translation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish regulatory levels\nand separate epigenetic observations from DNA variation and evidence of\ncausation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Muscle and nerve cells of the same organism possess largely the same\nDNA, but produce different RNA and protein quantities. Their\nspecialisation depends in part on stable and reversible regulatory\nprocesses.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"regulatory-levels\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Regulatory levels<\/h3>\n<p>In bacteria, operons enable coordinated transcription of multiple\ngenes. Repressors may reduce initiation, while activators may promote\nit; metabolites may affect these factors. Eukaryotic genes are regulated\nthrough promoters, enhancers, silencers and transcription factors, among\nother mechanisms. Enhancers can influence distant promoters through\nspatial contacts. Chromatin remodelers alter accessibility; RNA\nsplicing, RNA degradation and translation determine subsequent\nquantities. MicroRNAs can affect translation and stability of matching\nRNAs through protein complexes.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"epigenetics-and-stability\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Epigenetics and stability<\/h3>\n<p>DNA methylation, histone modifications and chromatin organisation may\nstabilise regulatory states. In mammals, methylation of certain CpG-rich\npromoter regions is frequently associated with reduced transcription,\nbut not all DNA methylation has the same effect. Histone acetylation is\noften associated with more accessible chromatin. Histone methylation has\ndifferent associations depending on the position. Some states persist\nthrough mitotic divisions. Transmission across generations is distinct;\nmany marks are reset during mammalian germ-cell development and early\nembryogenesis. Epigenetic differences are not general proof that\nacquired experiences are inherited.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"measurement-and-interpretation\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measurement and\ninterpretation<\/h3>\n<p>RNA amounts, chromatin accessibility and modifications measure\ndifferent levels. A statistical association between a mark and\nexpression leaves open cause, consequence and common influences. Time\nseries, controlled comparisons and targeted functional tests can assess\ncompeting explanations.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_033_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Simulated approach to steady-state RNA abundance from M(0)=0 at equal synthesis rate and two degradation constants.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Simulated approach to steady-state RNA\nabundance from M(0)=0 at equal synthesis rate and two degradation\nconstants.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Simulated approach to steady-state RNA abundance from\nM(0)=0 at equal synthesis rate and two degradation constants.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>In a teaching model for mRNA amount M,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dM/dt=s-kM<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere s is a synthesis rate and k a degradation constant. With constant\nparameters the steady state is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M^*=s/k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor s = 20 molecules/h and k = 0.5/h, the mean is 40 molecules. Doubling\nk at constant s reduces the steady state to 20. The half-life without\nnew synthesis is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.39<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln(2)/k\\approx1.39<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nh for k = 0.5/h.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate M* for s = 30/h and k = 0.5/h.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can the same RNA amount result from different synthesis and\ndegradation rates?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does a negative correlation between promoter methylation and RNA\nabundance establish direct inhibition?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">60 molecules.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The ratio s/k may be equal for different absolute rates.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Alternative causes, cell composition and reverse causality must\nbe considered.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The continuous model describes mean amounts, not individual discrete\nmolecules. It ignores feedback, cell division and time-varying\nsynthesis. Epigenetic mechanisms depend on organism and location; a\nuniversal activation or repression symbol would mislead.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Gene activity explains part of trait variation. M6-L7 connects\nmolecular alleles to their statistical distribution in offspring.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L7<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/16-1-regulation-of-gene-expression\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 16.1 Regulation of Gene Expression<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Regulatory levels\n· Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/16-3-eukaryotic-epigenetic-gene-regulation\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 16.3 Eukaryotic Epigenetic Gene Regulation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chromatin\naccessibility · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Epigenetics\">NHGRI\n– Epigenetics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Epigenome and inheritance · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/16-6-eukaryotic-translational-and-post-translational-gene-regulation\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 16.6 Eukaryotic Translational and Post-translational Gene\nRegulation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Protein regulation · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M6-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M6-L7 – Mendelian inheritance and linkage<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Molecular Genetics and Gene Regulation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L2<\/a>: recombination; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L5<\/a>: alleles; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L2<\/a>: probability.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate genotype probabilities\nfor simple crosses and recognise linked genes from recombinant\noffspring.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Molecular genetics explains the physical basis of alleles. Classical\ngenetics additionally asks how likely these alleles and associated\ntraits are to occur in offspring.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"segregation-and-traits\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Segregation and traits<\/h3>\n<p>Diploid organisms typically possess two alleles at an autosomal\nlocus. During regular meiosis homologous chromosomes separate; in the\nsimple model a heterozygous Aa individual produces A and a gametes in\nequal proportions. Random fertilisation between two heterozygotes yields\nAA, Aa and aa in a 1:2:1 ratio. With complete dominance of A, three\nquarters exhibit the dominant phenotype. With incomplete dominance or\ncodominance, the heterozygous phenotype may be distinct. Dominance\ndescribes the phenotype of a heterozygote, not allele frequency or\nevolutionary value. Penetrance is the fraction of genotype carriers\ndisplaying a defined phenotype; expressivity describes its\nmanifestation.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"linkage-and-recombination\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Linkage and recombination<\/h3>\n<p>Independent assortment holds in the simple model for unlinked loci.\nTwo loci on the same chromosome can be transmitted together. Crossing\nover between homologous chromatids can form new allele combinations. In\na double heterozygote AB/ab, AB and ab are parental and Ab and aB are\nrecombinant. A testcross with ab/ab reveals gamete types of the\nheterozygous parent under idealised conditions. The recombination\nfraction r is the fraction of recombinant offspring and ranges from 0 to\n0.5 in the usual model. For short distances, 100r approximates map\ndistance in centimorgans. Multiple crossovers may go undetected;\nphysical and genetic distances are not proportional. Epistasis can also\nalter phenotype ratios without changing allele segregation itself.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_034_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Simulated testcross: 420 AB, 400 ab, 90 Ab and 90 aB; the recombination fraction is 0.18.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Simulated testcross: 420 AB, 400 ab, 90\nAb and 90 aB; the recombination fraction is 0.18.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Simulated testcross: 420 AB, 400 ab, 90 Ab and 90 aB; the\nrecombination fraction is 0.18.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>An artificial testcross yields offspring AB: 420, ab: 400, Ab: 90 and\naB: 90. Of 1,000 offspring, 180 are recombinant;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>180<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1000<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.18<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=180/1000=0.18<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA short-distance approximation gives 18 cM. This approximation may\nunderestimate genetic distance when multiple crossovers occur. The\nfigure shows absolute counts, not measured chromosome lengths.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the probability of aa from Aa × Aa under the stated\nassumptions?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate r for 60 recombinants among 500 offspring.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can r = 0.5 prove that two genes lie on different chromosomes?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/2\\cdot1/2=1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>60<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>500<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60/500=0.12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\napproximately 12 cM for a short distance.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Distant loci on the same chromosome can also appear to assort\nalmost independently.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The testcross is theoretical. It assumes equal viability, reliable\ngenotype classification and regular segregation. Sex-linked,\nmitochondrial and polygenic inheritance require further models; most\nhuman traits cannot be explained by simple dominance schemes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The molecular and classical foundations of M6 recur in population\ngenetics and evolution. M7 introduces plant organisation and\ndiversity.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L1<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/12-3-laws-of-inheritance\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 12.3 Laws of Inheritance<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Segregation, independent\nassortment, linkage and epistasis · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary\">NHGRI – Talking\nGlossary of Genetic Terms<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Homologous recombination and genetic\nvariation · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
